"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 15. The surgical findings included a pathological stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension and positive lymphovascular invasion, alongside a Gleason 3+4 diagnosis. Countervailing factors include negative surgical margins and non-invasion of the seminal vesicles. The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of nodal sampling introduce uncertainty, as pNx does not equate to pN0. Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 3+3 to 3+4 over time. The primary uncertainty lies in the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as follow-up data is missing. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and trends would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
